# Cart API

`game.js` imports nothing. All state lives in what `init` returns, passed back as `s`.

```js
// @ts-check
/** @typedef {{ x: number, sc: Arcade.Lib.Score, flow: Arcade.Lib.Flow }} State */
/** @type {Arcade.Cart<State>} */
export default {
  init(g) { return { x: g.W / 2, /* ... */ }; },
  update(g, s) { if (g.btn("right")) s.x += 2; },
  draw(g, s) { g.cls(g.colors.night); g.text(`SCORE ${s.sc.score}`, 4, 4, g.colors.white); },
  probe(g, s) { return { W: g.W, H: g.H, x: s.x }; },        // what checks see; plain JSON; never changes state
  poke(g, s, o) { if (o.lives) s.sc.lives = o.lives; },       // optional test setup; never runs in play
};
```

Types for JSDoc: `Arcade.Lib.Grid Shape Piece Box Thing Rule Hit Timer Repeat Score Flow Turns Pop`. The type-check catches a misspelled button or sound.

**Clock:** 60 Hz; `update` then `draw`. `g.frame` counts frames but doesn't restart with a new game, so count play frames in `s`.

**Input:** `g.btn(b, p?)` held, `g.btnp(b, p?)` pressed this frame, `g.btnr(b, p?)` released this frame. `b` is `up down left right a b x y start`; `p` defaults to 1. `g.players` is controllers: 1 on the cabinet. ★ belongs to the cabinet and never reaches a cart.

**Screen:** `g.W` × `g.H` is 256×192 (never write the numbers). Fractions are floored; everything clips.
- `g.cls(c?)`, `g.pset(x, y, c)`, `g.pget(x, y)`, `g.line(x0, y0, x1, y1, c)`
- `g.rect(x, y, w, h, c)`, `g.rectfill(x, y, w, h, c)`, `g.circ(x, y, r, c)`, `g.circfill(x, y, r, c)`
- `g.text(s, x, y, c?, { scale?, align? })` returns its width; `align` is `left center right`; `g.textWidth(s, scale?)`. Glyphs are 5×7, 6 apart, lines 9 apart. Printable ASCII only: no ★ or ♥ in text.
- `g.sprite(rows)` once in `init`, `g.spr(sp, x, y, { flipX?, flipY?, scale?, color? })`. See the `sprites` guide.
- `g.camera(x?, y?)` offsets later drawing; `g.clip(x?, y?, w?, h?)` limits it. No arguments resets.

**Colors,** `g.colors.<name>`, or 0–31: 0 black, 1 night, 2 slate, 3 gray, 4 silver, 5 white, 6 maroon, 7 red, 8 coral, 9 pink, 10 brown, 11 orange, 12 tan, 13 peach, 14 gold, 15 yellow, 16 forest, 17 green, 18 lime, 19 mint, 20 teal, 21 aqua, 22 navy, 23 blue, 24 sky, 25 ice, 26 indigo, 27 purple, 28 lilac, 29 plum, 30 magenta, 31 rose.

**Random:** `g.rand()` in [0, 1), `g.rnd(n)` an integer below n, `g.pick(list)`. Seeded, so tests repeat.

**Sound:** `g.sfx(name, { pitch?, vol?, voice? })`: `blip select jump coin pop hit explode powerup lose win`. `g.tone({ note: "C5", wave, frames, vol, slide, voice })`, `wave` is `square pulse triangle saw noise`; `g.stop(voice?)`. 4 voices.

**Saves:** `g.save(data)`, `g.load()`: one JSON value, at most 4 KB. For high scores use `g.lib.score`.

**Assets:** none needed; carts draw with shapes and sprites.
# checks.js

One async check per `(auto)` rule, keyed by rule id, written from the spec's words. A check passes by returning and fails by throwing, with a message saying what was expected and what happened. Each gets a fresh cart: seed 1, nothing saved. The bot below is tested, so don't write your own loops.

```js
// @ts-check
const chase = (t) => (p) => t.bot.toward(p.player, t.bot.nearest(p.player, p.fish) ?? p.player, { axis: "x" });

/** @type {Arcade.Checks} */
export default {
  async R1(t) {                                   // R1: holding right moves 3 pixels per frame
    const a = await t.bot.start();
    t.bot.eq((await t.bot.hold("right", 10)).player.x, a.player.x + 30, "x after 10 frames");
  },
  async R3(t) {                                   // R3: catching a fish scores 10
    await t.bot.start();
    await t.poke({ lives: 99 });                  // the rule is about score, not lives
    const p = await t.bot.reach(chase(t), (q) => q.caught >= 1, "a caught fish");
    t.bot.eq(p.score, 10 * p.caught, "score");
  },
};
```

## `t.bot`

- `start(o?)`: title into play: waits the 30 ignored frames, presses START, checks `mode === "play"`. `o.button`, `o.mode`. Returns the probe, with **1 frame of play already run**, so count from its frame number, not 0.
- `hold(buttons, frames)`: hold (`"right"`, `"left up"`, `""`) for exact frames. Returns the probe, as do the next two.
- `press(button, then = 1)`: press for one frame, then run `then` more.
- `wait(frames)`: run with nothing held.
- `reach(steer, until, what, o?)`: every `o.every` frames (default 3) hold `steer(p)`, until `until(p)`. Returns the probe. Fails with "the bot couldn't get to <what>" if the mode changes (game over) or `o.max` frames pass (default 3000). `o.anyMode: true` keeps going across a mode change; use it when reaching the mode change itself is the goal.
- `play(steer, { until, max, every, anyMode })`: the same, but returns `{ p, frames, reached }` and never fails.
- `delta(get, { frames = 1, held = "" })`: how much `get(probe)` changes.
- `toward(from, to, { axis, dead = 2 })`, `away(from, threat, { axis })`: the buttons to hold, as a string. Things with `w`/`h` count from their centers.
- `nearest(from, list)`: the closest thing, or null.
- `pathStep(open, from, to)`: first button of the shortest tile walk; `open(x, y)` says what's walkable.
- `ok(cond, why)`, `eq(got, want, what)`, `near(got, want, what, eps?)`: fail with a clear message. `eq` compares arrays and objects too.

## `t`

`t.probe()`, `t.step(n)`, `t.input([{ frames, p1: "right a" }])` (nothing moves until `step`), `t.sounds()` (`[{ frame, kind }]`), `t.frame()`, `t.reset({ seed?, keepSave?, players? })`. All async: `await` each. Best score: end a game, `await t.reset({ keepSave: true })`, probe `best`.

## `t.poke`: setting up, not testing

`await t.poke({ lives: 99 })` calls the cart's `poke(g, s, o)`. **Use it to keep a bot alive so a check can reach the late game.**
- Never poke what the rule is about: a rule about losing lives plays with real lives; a rule about score doesn't poke score.
- Keep the real thing happening: read a speed from real movement, not from a poked or probed number.

## Probe for the rules

Your checks decide what `probe()` must return, and `game.js` then provides it. Counters make checks short and strong: `caught: { fish, boot }`, `landed: { fish, boot }`, `played`, `kinds`. Give things an `id` so a check can follow one across frames. Check accounting across the rule's words: `lives === 99 - landed.fish - caught.boot`.

## Limits and speed

10 s per check on node, 30 s in the browser, 18,000 frames. In the browser each look at the game is slow, so stay under about 1,000 looks per check: with nothing to steer, use `every: 10` or `wait`. Rule ids must match the spec's `(auto)` rules exactly.
# spec.md

The spec is the source of truth. A template's `spec.md` shows the format; keep its headings, in order:

`# Title`, then `maker: A · with: — · parent: — · session: —`, then `## Pitch`, `## Rules`, `## Controls`, `## Look & sound`, `## Assumptions` (`- A1: …`), `## Changelog` (`- v1: …`).

Rules are one list item each (longer ones continue on lines indented two spaces), and nothing else goes in that section:

```md
- R1 (auto): Holding right moves the frog 2 pixels per frame; it stops at the right edge.
- R2 (you check): The frog looks happy when it catches a fly.
```

Ids never change and are never reused. `(auto)`: the playtest proves it by pressing buttons, stepping frames and reading state. `(you check)`: a person judges looks, feelings and fun.

**An auto rule names what happens, when (in frames, 60 per second), and what's visible in the game's state.**

| Bad | Good |
|---|---|
| You can catch the fruit. | When the basket touches a fruit, it disappears that frame and the score goes up by 10. |
| It gets harder over time. | The rocks fall 1 pixel per frame faster every 600 frames, up to 6. |
| The game ends when you lose. | At 0 lives the game-over screen shows within 30 frames. |
| A fruit falls about every 2 seconds. | A new fruit starts falling every 120 frames of play. |
| The high score is saved. | After a game over and a restart, the title shows the best score so far. |
| There are sparkles when you score. | (you check) Sparkles burst from the goal when you score. |

- Split a rule that does two things, like "scores 100 and plays a pop".
- Use exact numbers. If the kid didn't say, pick one, write it in the rule, and add an Assumption.
- Prefer patterns over luck when a rule is about how often: "every 5th thing is a rock" can be proved.
- Rules are read aloud on the plan screen, so keep them short and plain. None about the code.

# g.lib.score: points, lives, saved high score

A score is `{ score, lives, best, newBest, key }`. `g.save()` holds one JSON value per cart; these treat it as an object with named fields, so a high score doesn't overwrite other saved things.

```js
s.sc = score.newScore(g, { lives: 3 });       // in init and at each new game
score.addPoints(s.sc, 100);
if (score.loseLife(s.sc) === 0) { score.commit(g, s.sc); flow.go(s.flow, "over"); }
```

### `score.newScore(g, opts?)`
A fresh score. `opts.lives` defaults to 3; `opts.key` (default `"best"`) is the saved field, so use another key for a separate high score per mode.

### `score.addPoints(sc, points)`
Mutates. Adds (never below 0), raises `best` and sets `newBest` when passed. Doesn't save. Returns the score.

### `score.loseLife(sc, n?)`
Mutates. Takes away 1 or `n`, not below 0. Returns lives left.

### `score.gainLife(sc, n?, max?)`
Mutates. Adds 1 or `n`, capped at `max`. Returns lives.

### `score.isOver(sc)`
True at no lives.

### `score.commit(g, sc)`
Saves the high score if it beats the saved one, keeping other fields; returns true if it saved. Call once when the game ends. Checks test it with `t.reset({ keepSave: true })`.

### `score.loadField(g, key, fallback?)`
The saved field, or `fallback` (default null).

### `score.saveField(g, key, value)`
Saves one field, keeping the rest. Throws over 4 KB. Call on events, not every frame.
# g.lib.fx: pop-ups, life hearts, title, banner

Screen polish that changes no game state. Keep pop-ups in `s.pops = []`.

```js
fx.tick(s.pops);                                  // top of update, in every mode
fx.pop(s.pops, "+10", thing.x, thing.y, c.yellow); // on an event
// draw:
const art = fx.title(g, "APPLE CATCH", s.sc.best); // title screen
g.spr(s.art.apple, art.cx - 18, art.cy - 18, { scale: 3 });
fx.lives(g, s.sc.lives, g.W - 2, 1);
fx.draw(g, s.pops);
if (s.flow.mode === "over") fx.banner(g, "GAME OVER", s.sc.newBest ? "NEW BEST!" : `SCORE ${s.sc.score}`);
```

### `fx.pop(pops, text, x, y, color, frames?)`
Mutates. Adds a pop-up centered on x that drifts up for `frames` frames (default 30).

### `fx.tick(pops)`
Mutates. One frame; finished pop-ups go. Call once per update.

### `fx.draw(g, pops)`
Draws them, kept on screen.

### `fx.lives(g, n, x, y, color?)`
`n` hearts, 7×6 and 9 apart, right edge at x, top at y; default red, at most 12.

### `fx.title(g, name, best, opts?)`
The whole title's words: name near the top (smaller if long), blinking PRESS START and BEST near the bottom. `opts.color` for the name, `opts.ink` for the small words. Returns `{ cx, cy, h }`, the center and height of the clear space between them: **draw title art there so it can't cover the words.**

### `fx.banner(g, text, sub?, color?)`
A panel mid-screen with big words and an optional line under them.
# g.lib.flow: title → play → game over

A flow is `{ mode, t, prev }`: the current screen (any string), frames since entering it, and the screen before. Switch on `s.flow.mode` in `update` and `draw`; probe `mode`.

```js
flow.tick(s.flow);
if (s.flow.mode !== "play") {
  if (flow.startPressed(g, s.flow)) {
    if (s.flow.mode === "title") newGame(g, s);
    flow.go(s.flow, s.flow.mode === "title" ? "play" : "title");
  }
  return;
}
```

### `flow.newFlow(mode?)`
A flow on `mode` (default `"title"`).

### `flow.go(flow, mode)`
Mutates. Switch to `mode`, set `prev`, reset `t`. You reset the game state yourself.

### `flow.tick(flow)`
Mutates. Count one frame; call once at the top of `update`. Returns `t`.

### `flow.startPressed(g, flow, opts?)`
Which player pressed to start (1, 2…), or 0. Presses in the first `opts.after` frames (default 30) are ignored, so checks wait 31 frames (`t.bot.start` does). `opts.buttons` defaults to START and A; `opts.players` to every controller.
# Sprites

`g.sprite(rows)` makes a sprite once, in `init`; `g.spr(sp, x, y, { flipX, flipY, scale, color })` draws it. One character per pixel: `.` is transparent, `0`–`9` and `a`–`v` are colors 0–31 (`a` 10 brown, `b` 11 orange, `e` 14 gold, `f` 15 yellow, `h` 17 green, `n` 23 blue, `o` 24 sky). See `cart-api` for all 32.

**Every row must be the same length, or `g.sprite` throws.** So draw once and get it right:
- Count on a fixed width: pick 8 or 12 or 16 and write each row to it. Don't pad or slice rows in code.
- Check before moving on, without running the game: `node -e 'for (const r of ["..11..",".1111."]) console.log(r.length)'`.
- Keep rows in one constant in `game.js`, and redraw by replacing that constant, not the file.

**What reads at this size.** The screen is 256×192 on a 10" panel. A thing players must tell apart at a glance is 12–16 pixels, with one strong silhouette and color each. An 8×8 thing is a blob: fine for a bullet, not for a fish that must not look like a boot. Use a dark outline or a background color that contrasts with everything moving. For a big title-screen picture use `scale: 3` on the same sprite. `flipX` gives you facing left and right from one sprite; two frames swapped on `g.frame % 16 < 8` give a walk.

```js
const CAT = ["..1...1.", ".1f1.1f1", ".1fffff1", ".1f1f1f1", ".1fffff1", "..11111.", "..1f1f1.", "..1.1.1."];
// in init: s.cat = g.sprite(CAT);   in draw: g.spr(s.cat, s.x, s.y, { flipX: s.left, scale: 2 });
```

Plain shapes (`circfill`, `rectfill`) are cheapest and often enough for backgrounds and small things.
// @ts-check
/** @typedef {{ flow: Arcade.Lib.Flow, sc: Arcade.Lib.Score, ship: Arcade.Lib.Thing, things: Arcade.Lib.Thing[], spawn: Arcade.Lib.Timer, safe: Arcade.Lib.Timer, tick: Arcade.Lib.Timer, played: number, pops: Arcade.Lib.Pop[], art: Record<string, Arcade.Sprite> }} State */

// Dodge template: free movement, things fly at you, stay alive. Change CFG and draw.
const CFG = { lives: 3, speed: 2, spawnEvery: 40, safeFrames: 90, comet: 1, cometStep: 0.5, cometEvery: 600, cometMax: 3 };
const SIZE = 8; // ship and comet size in pixels
const TOP = 10; // the HUD band; nothing flies there
const SHIP = ["55......", ".oo5....", ".ooo55..", "boooooo5", "boooooo5", ".ooo55..", ".oo5....", "55......"];
const COMET = ["..bb.f..", ".bffb..f", "bf55fb..", "bf55fbf.", ".bffb.f.", "..bb...f", "........", "........"];

/** @param {Arcade.G} g @param {State} s */
function newGame(g, s) {
  const { score, timers } = g.lib;
  s.sc = score.newScore(g, { lives: CFG.lives });
  s.ship = { kind: "ship", x: 16, y: (g.H - SIZE) / 2, w: SIZE, h: SIZE };
  s.things = [];
  s.spawn = timers.timer(CFG.spawnEvery, true);
  s.tick = timers.timer(60, true);
  s.safe = timers.timer(CFG.safeFrames);
  s.safe.done = true; // not safe at the start
  s.played = 0;
}

/** Comet speed after `played` frames of play (R5). */
const speedAt = (/** @type {number} */ played) => Math.min(CFG.cometMax, CFG.comet + CFG.cometStep * Math.floor(played / CFG.cometEvery));

/** @type {Arcade.Cart<State>} */
export default {
  init(g) {
    const s = /** @type {State} */ ({ flow: g.lib.flow.newFlow() });
    s.pops = [];
    s.art = { ship: g.sprite(SHIP), comet: g.sprite(COMET) };
    newGame(g, s);
    return s;
  },

  update(g, s) {
    const { flow, timers, collide, score, fx } = g.lib;
    flow.tick(s.flow);
    fx.tick(s.pops);
    if (s.flow.mode !== "play") {
      if (flow.startPressed(g, s.flow)) {
        if (s.flow.mode === "title") newGame(g, s);
        flow.go(s.flow, s.flow.mode === "title" ? "play" : "title");
      }
      return;
    }

    const sh = s.ship;
    if (g.btn("left")) sh.x -= CFG.speed;
    if (g.btn("right")) sh.x += CFG.speed;
    if (g.btn("up")) sh.y -= CFG.speed;
    if (g.btn("down")) sh.y += CFG.speed;
    sh.x = Math.max(0, Math.min(g.W - SIZE, sh.x));
    sh.y = Math.max(TOP, Math.min(g.H - SIZE, sh.y));

    s.played += 1;
    if (timers.tick(s.tick)) score.addPoints(s.sc, 1);
    if (timers.tick(s.spawn)) s.things.push({ kind: "comet", x: g.W, y: TOP + g.rnd(g.H - TOP - SIZE), w: SIZE, h: SIZE });
    const v = speedAt(s.played);
    for (const c of s.things) if (c.kind === "comet") c.x -= v;

    timers.tick(s.safe);
    const hits = collide.resolve([sh, ...s.things], [{ a: "comet", b: "ship", kill: s.safe.done ? "a" : "none" }]);
    s.things = collide.alive(s.things).filter((c) => c.x > -SIZE);
    if (hits.length && s.safe.done) {
      g.sfx("hit");
      fx.pop(s.pops, "OUCH", sh.x + 4, sh.y - 8, g.colors.red);
      timers.restart(s.safe);
      if (score.loseLife(s.sc) === 0) {
        score.commit(g, s.sc);
        g.sfx("lose");
        flow.go(s.flow, "over");
        s.things = [];
      }
    }
  },

  draw(g, s) {
    const { fx } = g.lib;
    const c = g.colors;
    g.cls(c.night);
    for (let i = 0; i < 24; i++) g.pset((((i * 53 - (g.frame >> 1)) % g.W) + g.W) % g.W, (i * 37) % g.H, c.slate);
    if (s.flow.mode === "title") {
      const art = fx.title(g, "COMET DODGE", s.sc.best, { color: c.sky });
      g.spr(s.art.ship, art.cx - 16, art.cy - 16, { scale: 4 });
      return;
    }
    for (const t of s.things) g.spr(s.art.comet, t.x, t.y);
    const blink = !s.safe.done && g.frame % 8 < 4;
